#set par(justify: true, spacing: .8em)

*Recommendation.* Make no construction award under the current township-wide eligibility rule.
Approve a small address audit, request that the funding authority allow block-level eligibility,
and prepare a conditional award for county-owned backhaul that is leased to any qualified
provider.

*Why the grant cannot be spent today.* Each of the six townships contains at least one block
marked served, so none is eligible. North Ridge, West Plains and River Bend also fall short of
the modeled 25\% access floor at baseline. North Ridge has up to 1,531 households in
satellite-coded blocks that could be reclassified, but even a fully successful correction
leaves four flagged blocks and does not unlock funding under the current rule. The audit needed
to make the record costs about \$12,600 and takes three to nine months; it is the cheapest step
the county can take and it is required for any future award.

*What the rule change buys.* Under block eligibility and a 25\% floor, a county backhaul
partnership adds #value("ppp_hh") qualifying household opportunities at #value("ppp_cost") over
ten years, compared with #value("coop_hh") for a sole cooperative award (#value("advantage")
fewer) and #value("tender_hh") for a lowest-cost tender (#value("tender_gap") fewer). The
partnership costs about \$2,306 per household, the lowest of the three, and it remains first if
verified county evidence is allowed to override provider claims (#value("override_count")
opportunities).

*Who gains and who pays.* Residents gain more orderable connections, though retail price,
installation and device support still determine whether they are used. The cooperative can bid
for last-mile work and lease county capacity but no longer receives the whole allocation; the
cable provider can bid or lease on the same terms and receives no construction grant under the
sole award. The county bears four hub assets and renewal, vacancy and residual maintenance risk.
Discounted rents of #value("ppp_rent") cover #value("om_recovery") of the county's discounted
hub maintenance, leaving about \$44,981 plus the hub capital to fund from the budget.

*Conditions before construction.* Obtain written authority for the revised eligibility rule;
verify orderability, alignments, wireless serviceability and hub capacity; publish
non-discriminatory lease terms; and secure tenant commitments with a maintenance reserve. If
the rule change cannot be obtained, preserve the funds and keep the audit current. If verified
address evidence becomes acceptable, reopen the mechanism comparison with the new eligibility
map. Test whether household service supports work and school at the times people need it, and
report the results to this Board annually.

*Decision requested.* (1) Approve a \$12,600 address audit and authorize staff to request a
block-level eligibility interpretation from the funding authority. (2) Authorize preparation
of a conditional open-access award, with construction released only after (1) is resolved.
(3) Reserve a maintenance contingency for hub assets and report lease performance at each
renewal.

*Next 90 days.* Commission the audit and sample at least 10 addresses per township stratum.
Collect order denials, quoted prices, installation dates and technology types. Issue a
non-discriminatory lease term sheet and solicit indicative bids from the cooperative and the
cable provider. Complete a fixed-wireless serviceability survey in the two hardest-terrain
townships, and return to this Board with a revised eligibility map and an award schedule.

*Principal risks.* Lease vacancy, construction-cost overruns and a rejected challenge would
each delay the plan. The staged award, the maintenance reserve and the cooperative fallback
limit the county's exposure; none of them requires committing funds before the rule change.
